WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.993
Module 2: designing a survey
that answers the question.

00:00:03.993 --> 00:00:07.043
A survey is only as good as its questions.

00:00:07.043 --> 00:00:10.601
You'll plan from the
client's decision backwards,

00:00:10.601 --> 00:00:16.627
write items people can answer honestly,
and design it so nobody can be singled out.

00:00:16.627 --> 00:00:18.442
About forty-five minutes.

00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:22.949
Clients usually arrive with
questions they'd like to ask.

00:00:22.949 --> 00:00:26.683
Start somewhere else: with
the decision they'll make.

00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:31.543
Ask the sponsor what they'll do
differently depending on the answers.

00:00:31.543 --> 00:00:35.066
Then ask what they need to
know to make that call.

00:00:35.066 --> 00:00:38.588
Those become your objectives,
usually two to four.

00:00:38.588 --> 00:00:41.758
Ask whose view matters,
what they'll compare,

00:00:41.758 --> 00:00:45.140
and what else people are
being asked to fill in.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.324
Then test every draft question.

00:00:47.324 --> 00:00:52.396
Which objective does it serve, and
what would anyone do with the answer?

00:00:52.396 --> 00:00:54.228
If nobody can say, cut it.

00:00:54.228 --> 00:00:59.441
Alex's huddle pulse had four objectives:
reach, value, barriers and voice.

00:00:59.441 --> 00:01:03.315
Every question in the final
survey maps to one of them.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.402
Put it on one page and agree it
with the sponsor before you build.

00:01:08.402 --> 00:01:13.073
Decision, objectives, audience and
size, the groups you'll compare,

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:18.023
how confidentiality works, length,
dates, how results will be reported,

00:01:18.023 --> 00:01:18.999
and the risks.

00:01:18.999 --> 00:01:23.810
For the huddle pulse, the big risk
was survey fatigue after February.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.503
Most weak questions
have one of six faults.

00:01:27.503 --> 00:01:30.482
Double-barrelled: two ideas, one answer.

00:01:30.482 --> 00:01:36.516
"The huddles are well run and the follow-up
is good." What does someone answer if

00:01:36.516 --> 00:01:40.315
the meeting's great but
nothing happens afterwards?

00:01:40.315 --> 00:01:40.985
Split it.

00:01:40.985 --> 00:01:43.145
Leading: wording that pushes.

00:01:43.145 --> 00:01:48.731
"How great have the huddles been?" Use
neutral wording on a balanced scale.

00:01:48.731 --> 00:01:51.562
Loaded or absolute: "always", "never".

00:01:51.562 --> 00:01:55.882
"Do you always attend huddles?"
Almost nobody can say yes.

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:59.308
Give a time frame instead:
"In the last month,

00:01:59.308 --> 00:02:03.629
have you taken part?" Jargon:
acronyms people don't share.

00:02:03.629 --> 00:02:07.278
Unanswerable: asking people
what they can't know,

00:02:07.278 --> 00:02:10.630
like how the merger
changed patient outcomes.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.461
Ask about their own recent experience.

00:02:13.461 --> 00:02:19.345
And identifying: a name, a tiny unit or
a unique detail in an anonymous survey.

00:02:19.345 --> 00:02:21.952
Remove it, or use broad categories.

00:02:22.467 --> 00:02:22.938
Try it.

00:02:22.938 --> 00:02:25.157
Match each question to its fault.

00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:27.649
Label every point on a scale.

00:02:27.649 --> 00:02:32.502
A five-point agreement scale, from
strongly disagree to strongly agree,

00:02:32.502 --> 00:02:33.801
is a sound default.

00:02:33.801 --> 00:02:37.902
Keep it balanced, with as many
positive options as negative.

00:02:37.902 --> 00:02:41.320
Keep one scale and one
direction within a section.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:45.626
Offer "not applicable" when some
people genuinely can't answer.

00:02:45.626 --> 00:02:51.026
If you include a negatively worded item,
mark it reverse scored in SurveyCraft,

00:02:51.026 --> 00:02:53.077
so it's flipped in the export.

00:02:53.077 --> 00:02:58.409
And when you want to compare with an
earlier survey, re-use the exact wording.

00:02:58.409 --> 00:03:01.690
Change one word and you've
changed the question.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.767
Pick the simplest type that
answers the objective.

00:03:05.767 --> 00:03:09.690
Multiple choice for one
answer, checkboxes for several.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:15.254
A labelled scale for ratings, or a matrix
for several items on the same scale,

00:03:15.254 --> 00:03:18.036
though big grids tire people on phones.

00:03:18.036 --> 00:03:20.390
Ranking when you need priorities.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:24.313
Text boxes for a short fact
or a story, used sparingly.

00:03:24.313 --> 00:03:27.095
Most pulses need little more than that.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.829
Keep open questions few,
optional and specific.

00:03:30.829 --> 00:03:35.844
"What one thing would make working
across sites easier?" beats "Any other

00:03:35.844 --> 00:03:40.860
comments?" Keep the survey short, and
tell people the real time it takes.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.333
Start with easy, relevant questions,

00:03:43.333 --> 00:03:48.829
put sensitive ones after people have warmed
up, and put demographics at the end,

00:03:48.829 --> 00:03:53.226
or attach them from a roster so
people don't answer them at all.

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:56.874
Demographic questions
let you compare groups.

00:03:56.874 --> 00:03:59.946
They're also how a small
group gets exposed.

00:03:59.946 --> 00:04:01.970
Ask only what you'll compare.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:07.275
Design your categories so the groups
you care about stay above SurveyCraft's

00:04:07.275 --> 00:04:08.601
minimum group size.

00:04:08.601 --> 00:04:10.695
Combine small groups up front,

00:04:10.695 --> 00:04:14.953
so four clinic managers become
part of "supervisor or above".

00:04:14.953 --> 00:04:17.466
Use choice questions, not free text.

00:04:17.466 --> 00:04:23.190
Riley's draft listed twenty-three units,
four of them with fewer than five people.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:27.169
The final huddle pulse asks
department, with two options,

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:32.055
and a role level with four, and raises
the minimum group size to five.

00:04:32.567 --> 00:04:34.739
Three numbers, explained simply.

00:04:34.739 --> 00:04:39.085
Response rate is people who
finished, divided by people invited.

00:04:39.085 --> 00:04:43.974
The huddle pulse got two hundred and
thirty-one out of three hundred and

00:04:43.974 --> 00:04:46.283
ninety-two, so fifty-nine percent.

00:04:46.283 --> 00:04:51.036
Margin of error is how far a percentage
could be from the true figure.

00:04:51.036 --> 00:04:56.535
With about two hundred and thirty answers,
it's roughly plus or minus six points.

00:04:56.535 --> 00:05:01.560
And group size: small groups swing a
lot, so don't build a finding on one.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:02.579
One more thing.

00:05:02.579 --> 00:05:06.245
Statistically significant
isn't the same as important.

00:05:06.245 --> 00:05:11.881
In a big sample, a tiny difference can be
significant and mean nothing in practice.

00:05:12.367 --> 00:05:15.816
Every invitation makes a
promise, so make it true.

00:05:15.816 --> 00:05:20.645
Tell people why you're asking, that
it's voluntary, how long it takes,

00:05:20.645 --> 00:05:25.819
what's confidential and who sees which
results, and when they'll hear back.

00:05:25.819 --> 00:05:31.200
And never write "completely anonymous"
until you've checked that the settings,

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.201
the demographics and the way
you send it all back that up.

00:05:35.201 --> 00:05:37.340
Module 4 shows you exactly how.

00:05:37.833 --> 00:05:40.903
Here's Riley's first
draft of the huddle pulse.

00:05:40.903 --> 00:05:44.364
Find what you'd change,
write a fix for each problem,

00:05:44.364 --> 00:05:46.780
and name the single most serious one.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.066
Then compare with the model answer.

00:05:49.567 --> 00:05:51.839
Quick check before you start building.

00:05:52.333 --> 00:05:53.064
To sum up.

00:05:53.064 --> 00:05:54.819
Start from the decision.

00:05:54.819 --> 00:05:59.132
Write single, neutral, answerable
items on labelled scales.

00:05:59.132 --> 00:06:03.007
Design demographics that
keep every group big enough.

00:06:03.007 --> 00:06:07.905
Report response rate, and don't
confuse significant with important.

00:06:07.905 --> 00:06:09.879
And make your promise true.

00:06:09.879 --> 00:06:13.022
Next, Module 3: building
it in SurveyCraft.

